 ## Overview
-Training Ray Compositing (Sigma) composites per-ray samples of density ($\sigma$) and color ($\mathbf{c}$) into final image colors, alpha weights, and depth, while simultaneously computing gradients with respect to the input parameters for training [S1, unofficial]. It extends standard alpha compositing by including ambient light contributions and supporting a custom backward pass to propagate gradients from the loss function back to the density and color fields [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its sibling [Training Ray Compositing (Backward)](3893-training-ray-compositing-backward.md) by handling the forward composition and the joint forward-backward logic in a unified CUDA kernel interface, whereas the backward variant focuses specifically on the gradient computation logic [S1, unofficial].
+Training Ray Compositing (Sigma) is a differentiable volume rendering operation that composites per-ray samples of density ($\sigma$), view-dependent color ($\text{RGB}$), and ambient light contributions into final pixel colors, accumulated alpha weights, and depth values [S1, unofficial]. It is specifically designed for the training loop of neural radiance fields, providing the forward pass for image reconstruction loss and the necessary intermediate values for the backward pass to compute gradients with respect to $\sigma$ and $\text{RGB}$ parameters [S1, unofficial]. It extends standard alpha compositing by supporting ambient light terms and custom backward kernels for efficient gradient propagation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The forward pass computes the accumulated color, alpha, and depth, while the backward pass computes the gradients of the loss with respect to the input densities and colors [S1, unofficial]. The implementation relies on contiguous CUDA tensors [S1, unofficial].
+The method performs alpha compositing along rays, accumulating transparency and weighted color/depth contributions. It supports an additional ambient light term per sample. The implementation relies on a CUDA backend kernel for the forward pass and a separate kernel for the backward pass [S1, unofficial].
 
